---
// THE PRODUCT PAGE.
//
// WHAT WAS WRONG. It opened "ORMAS is one product. These are its seven features," which defines
// the product AS the seven — so a reader arrived at a page about seven things rather than a page
// about one architecture. Every section after it inherited that error: a capability grid framed as
// "what the seven are built from", a feature list framed as the product surface, and nothing at
// all introducing what ORMAS actually is or what class of problem it exists to solve.
//
// THE CORRECTION. ORMAS is one training architecture with a broad capability surface. Seven
// applications of it are proven, mapped to a market, and sold with confidence — but they are the
// seven we lead with, not the definition of the thing. Other combinations exist.
//
// SOURCES. The introduction is written from ormas_preprint/main.tex §Introduction (the opacity
// argument, the bounded 4-operation chain, the five telemetry layers, the flight-recorder
// framing), Commercial Plan `00` (addressability), `03 §2-3` (capabilities and the matrix), `07`
// (the record, the bound table, the diff, signing), `06 §5` (the read-only mode) and `18 §5` (the
// licence). Nothing here is invented.
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import PageBanner from '../components/PageBanner.astro';
import GetInTouch from '../components/GetInTouch.astro';
import Button from '../components/Button.astro';
import Eyebrow from '../components/Eyebrow.astro';
import ChainDiagram from '../components/ChainDiagram.astro';
import { features } from '../data/features';
import { sectors } from '../data/sectors';

// The breadth of the product, as concrete capabilities rather than abstractions. Every line is a
// property the architecture has today, stated in the register of somebody who has to deploy and
// answer for it rather than build it.
const surface = [
	{
		title: 'Five layers of telemetry',
		body: 'From the health of the whole system down to the attribution of a single parameter. The network computes these as it trains; switching them off would take extra work rather than save any.',
	},
	{
		title: 'Health for every component, at every step',
		body: 'Not sampled and not periodic. Each component is measured continuously against its own baseline, so a component drifting away from itself shows up while it is still drifting.',
	},
	{
		title: 'Eight named failure conditions',
		body: 'A component can die, oscillate, saturate, stagnate, explode, lose confidence, lose its gradient, or fail in a way the system declines to classify. Each one has a name and a declared ceiling on the response.',
	},
	{
		title: 'Corrections capped before they happen',
		body: 'Nothing is altered by more than a stated fraction of that component\'s own weight. Every change is balanced so it adds nothing on net, and whatever is left over is written down.',
	},
	{
		title: 'A read-only mode',
		body: 'Full diagnostics, zero modification. Most institutions want to watch a system for a year before they let it touch a model, and this is the mode that lets them.',
	},
	{
		title: 'A record you can diff',
		body: 'Hashed, exportable, and comparable line by line against the version you last approved. Reviewers rarely ask what your model is. They ask what changed.',
	},
	{
		title: 'Runs where your data already is',
		body: 'No hosted service, no metering, nothing leaving your network. Inside a hospital enclave or a bank, this is what decides whether the conversation happens at all.',
	},
];

// Which markets each application is aimed at, and which one it leads. Read from data/sectors.ts so
// the mapping cannot drift from the sector pages.
const marketsFor = (id: string) => sectors.filter((s) => s.features.includes(id));
const leadFor = (id: string) => sectors.find((s) => s.primaryFeature === id);
---

<Page title="ORMAS, the product" description={"One architecture, one licence, seven applications. Every model it trains arrives with the evidence of how it was made."}>
	<PageBanner
		eyebrow="THE PRODUCT"
		title="Every model it trains arrives with the evidence of how it was made."
		trail={[{ label: 'Home', href: '/' }, { label: 'Product' }]}
	/>

	<!-- 1. WHAT IT IS. The page had no such section: it went from a banner straight to a capability
	     grid, which asks a reader to absorb a taxonomy before knowing what the thing is. -->
	<section class="section container">
		<h2 class="section-title">Each component stays addressable from the first step to the last.</h2>
		<ChainDiagram />
		<div class="prod-intro">
			<p>
				Train a model the ordinary way and the process erases itself. You finish with weights, a
				loss curve, and whatever the team still remembers. Six months on, a regulator asks what
				changed between the version you validated and the version you shipped, and every available
				answer is a reconstruction.
			</p>
			<p>
				The cause is not carelessness. In a standard network every error signal reaches every
				parameter on every step, so by the time training ends there is no component left that a
				question can be put to. The information was never lost. It was never separable in the
				first place.
			</p>
			<p>
				ORMAS bounds each component's learning path so the component stays individually
				addressable throughout the run. Your data does not change. Your objective does not change.
				The model you deploy is the one you would have deployed anyway. You also finish with a
				record of what happened inside it, and with parts you can point at by name.
			</p>
			<p class="prod-intro-turn">
				Naming is the whole thing. A part that has a name can be watched while it trains,
				protected when it starts to fail, corrected inside a limit you set in advance, tied back
				to the data that produced it, and taken out when a contract says it has to go.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<!-- 2. THE BREADTH. "It has many features" was asserted nowhere and demonstrated nowhere; the
	     page jumped straight to six abstractions. These are concrete and they are all shipping
	     properties of the architecture. -->
	<section class="band band--surface section reveal">
		<div class="container">
			<Eyebrow>WHAT THE ARCHITECTURE GIVES YOU</Eyebrow>
			<h2 class="section-title">All of it is produced while the model trains.</h2>
			<p class="section-lede">
				None of this is assembled afterwards out of logs. It falls out of the arithmetic the
				network is already doing, which is why it can be trusted and why it costs nothing extra to
				generate.
			</p>
			<div class="surface-list">
				{surface.map((s, i) => (
					<article class="surface-item">
						<div class="surface-label">
							<span class="surface-index tabular">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
							<h3>{s.title}</h3>
						</div>
						<p class="surface-body">{s.body}</p>
					</article>
				))}
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<!-- 3. THE SEVEN. Framed correctly: not what the product IS, but what it is proven at. -->
	<section class="section container reveal">
		<Eyebrow>WHERE WE LEAD</Eyebrow>
		<h2 class="section-title">Seven applications, worked out end to end.</h2>
		<p class="section-lede">
			Six operations on a named part — protect, repair, account, detect, isolate, remove — combine
			into a large number of useful things. We have not proven all of them and will not claim to
			have. Seven are finished, aimed at a market that is already paying for their absence, and
			backed by evidence we will show you. They appear in the order of what each one asks of you.
		</p>

		<ol class="applications">
			{features.map((f, i) => {
				const lead = leadFor(f.id);
				const serves = marketsFor(f.id);
				return (
					<li class="application">
						<div class="application-head">
							<p class="application-index tabular">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</p>
							<div>
								<h3><a href={`/product/${f.id}`}>{f.name}</a></h3>
								<p class="application-q">{f.question}</p>
							</div>
						</div>

						<div class="application-body">
							<p class="application-summary">{f.summary}</p>
							<dl class="application-meta">
								<div>
									<dt>Built from</dt>
									<dd class="application-caps">
										{f.capabilities.map((c) => <span>{c}</span>)}
									</dd>
								</div>
								<div>
									<dt>Asks of you</dt>
									<dd>{f.requires}</dd>
								</div>
								<div>
									<dt>{lead ? 'Leads in' : 'Serves'}</dt>
									<dd>
										{lead
											? `${lead.name}${serves.length > 1 ? ` · and ${serves.length - 1} more market${serves.length > 2 ? 's' : ''}` : ''}`
											: serves.length === sectors.length
												? 'All five markets'
												: serves.map((s) => s.name).join(' · ')}
									</dd>
								</div>
							</dl>
							<a class="application-link" href={`/product/${f.id}`}>Read this application →</a>
						</div>
					</li>
				);
			})}
		</ol>

		<p class="applications-note">
			Other combinations are possible and some of them are obvious. They are absent from this page
			because we would not yet sign a contract against them.
		</p>
	</section>

	<!-- 4. THE LICENCE. -->
	<section class="band band--dark section reveal">
		<div class="container">
			<Eyebrow>HOW YOU GET IT</Eyebrow>
			<h2 class="licence-title">There is no menu, and no per-application price.</h2>
			<div class="licence-cols">
				<p>
					One deployment licence covers the architecture and everything it can be asked to do.
					You turn on what your situation needs, and the scope goes into the contract rather than
					onto a meter. Annual, per institution, running inside your own environment.
				</p>
				<p>
					Beyond the software, the licence carries the parts that actually decide a regulated
					deployment: integration into your stack, limits calibrated for your domain, builds
					pinned to a version you can reproduce yourself, support, and indemnity. The research
					and its experiments stay published, so the mechanism can be checked by anyone who
					cares to.
				</p>
			</div>
			<Button variant="secondary" href="/licensing">How licensing works</Button>
		</div>
	</section>

	<GetInTouch variant="pre-book" heading="Start with the problem you already have." />
</Page>
